You'll lose protection wherever you try to put a [HoOA on. It won't prevent 
you from completing the quest wherever you try to put it on, provided you 
remove it first if you succeed.

So the only variable is really, do you have any protection? If you have, 
it's a slight risk.

Where did you find this helmet, and what types do you know already? If it 
was on a minetown guard or soldier corpse, and you haven't identified it, 
chances are very high it was a normal helmet, most likely +0 as these are 
frequenly generated as part of their inventory. If it was on a bones level, 
all bets are off. Otherwise, if my maths is correct, the chances are about 
9/((n - 1) * 78.8 + 9) that it will be a [HoOA, where n is the number of 
helmets you have not identified.

n                     1        2        3       4   
Chance of [HoOA      100%    10.25%    5.40%
Plain Helmet not IDd          6.41%    4.10%   3.02%

Plain helmets are more common in randomly generated items, which makes 
a [HoOA less likely if they are one of the identified types (n - 1 becomes 
n - 1/3 in the above formula).

I don't think that there are any guaranteed [HoOA.

>From this you could work out an expected increase in AC from trying it on, 
if you knew the mean enchantment of uncursed armour.

So unless it's from a bones file or [HoOA are the only unidentified 
helmets, the chances are good that it isn't one.

I'd say, it's worthwhile trying it if your naked AC is above 0 and you want 
ESP or don't have enchanted headgear, or in any case if you don't have 
protection.

Or you could wait till you find another helmet; the more visored helmets 
you find non-cursed, the less likely it's a [HoOA (unless you find 10 
cursed ones for each non-cursed!)


